            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I am afraid, this is not how operators (and `sa` is a regular operator) in Neovim work. They always assume that next typed characters define textobject or motion to act upon. So in this case Neovim will treat `\"` as textobject/motion and stop early because it (presumably) doesn't know textobject/motion with that start with that character.\r\n\r\nThe best you can do is to create custom Normal mode mappings for some common surroundings individually. So something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- **Do not** forget the `remap = true` part, it makes it treat keys \"as if typed by user\"\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'sa\"', 'saiw\"', { remap = true })\r\n```",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I am afraid, this is not how operators (and <code class=\"notranslate\">sa</code> is a regular operator) in Neovim work. They always assume that next typed characters define textobject or motion to act upon. So in this case Neovim will treat <code class=\"notranslate\">\"</code> as textobject/motion and stop early because it (presumably) doesn't know textobject/motion with that start with that character.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The best you can do is to create custom Normal mode mappings for some common surroundings individually. So something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"-- **Do not** forget the `remap = true` part, it makes it treat keys &quot;as if typed by user&quot;\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'sa&quot;', 'saiw&quot;', { remap = true })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">--</span> **Do not** forget the `remap = true` part, it makes it treat keys \"as if typed by user\"</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>n<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>sa\"<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>saiw\"<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, { <span class=\"pl-smi\">remap</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">true</span> })</pre></div>",
            "bodyText": "I am afraid, this is not how operators (and sa is a regular operator) in Neovim work. They always assume that next typed characters define textobject or motion to act upon. So in this case Neovim will treat \" as textobject/motion and stop early because it (presumably) doesn't know textobject/motion with that start with that character.\nThe best you can do is to create custom Normal mode mappings for some common surroundings individually. So something like this:\n-- **Do not** forget the `remap = true` part, it makes it treat keys \"as if typed by user\"\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'sa\"', 'saiw\"', { remap = true })",
